# [Domain Name] Agent

> This file is the **domain kernel** — the slim, harness-loaded entry surface.
> Sections marked `<!-- generated:NAME -->` are produced by
> `mdllm domain-kernel .` through the manual launch route below and **must not be
> hand-edited** (the pre-commit coherence check fails on drift). Everything
> outside those blocks is authored and preserved verbatim across regenerations.

> **Manual CLI launch — read before Session Start.** In this file, `mdllm
> <command>` is notation for the framework CLI, not an assumption that an
> executable named `mdllm` is installed. On Windows PowerShell and Codex managed
> shells, run `& "[framework-root]/tools/mdllm.ps1" <command>` even when
> `python` exists: a visible or harness-bundled interpreter may lack PyYAML, and
> the launcher selects one only after dependency-probing it. Elsewhere, use
> `python [framework-root]/tools/mdllm.py <command>` only with an interpreter
> that can `import yaml`. Interpret older bare-Python or `mdllm` instructions in
> generated blocks and copied prompts through this route. The framework path is
> rendered into this body because harness injection may omit YAML frontmatter.
> Automatic startup uses plain `estate-sync` and stays non-interactive and
> best-effort. If an operator explicitly requests a fresh manual estate sync,
> run `mdllm estate-sync . --require-fresh`; cached or unresolved state then
> exits nonzero so a restricted Codex task can request one-command
> network/filesystem approval and rerun the exact wrapper command. A sandbox
> denial is not evidence that the repository's Git credentials are invalid.

